WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.140
Let u be odd and greater than one, and
let I contain its distinct prime divisors.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.820
Use the prime roots of
unity chosen in Node four.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.360
First form B by adjoining i and
these roots to the integers.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:16.780
Enlarge B to A by adjoining one over u.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.340
Thus powers of u are allowed denominators.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:25.280
For each subset S, multiply its
chosen roots to obtain zeta sub S.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:31.740
Write c sub S, d sub S, and g
sub S for the three evaluations.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:34.480
All belong to this one ring.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:39.500
The evaluated norm identities say
that x sub S times its complex

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.360
conjugate equals u squared,
for any of these three values.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.640
Conjugation stays in B: each root
of unity becomes its inverse.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:52.740
Since u is invertible in A,
divide the conjugate by u squared.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.120
Multiplying this expression by
x sub S gives one. This is an

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.260
explicit inverse in A, more than just
nonvanishing in the complex plane.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.760
For u equal to three, the ring
is the integers with i, omega,

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:12.000
and one third adjoined. The inverse of c
of omega is its conjugate divided by nine.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.440
The same formula works for
every subset and for d and g.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.940
Now reduce A modulo one plus i.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:24.120
Because two equals the product of one
plus i and one minus i, two becomes zero.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.660
We must still prove that one
stays different from zero.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.360
The generators of B are roots of unity.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.340
Their powers give finitely many
monomials spanning B over the integers.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:40.760
Also B sits inside the complex
numbers, so it has no additive torsion.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:45.100
Hence B is a nonzero free
abelian group of finite rank.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.900
Its quotient by twice B is nonzero.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.780
Choose a maximal ideal
of B containing two.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.680
Its residue field has characteristic two.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:01.260
In this field, the square of one
plus i equals two i, which is zero.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:07.240
A field has no nonzero element with
square zero, so one plus i itself is zero.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:11.220
The odd integer u,
however, has residue one.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:17.280
We can therefore extend the map from
B to A, sending one over u to one.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:22.180
It still maps onto the nonzero
residue field and kills one plus i.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.760
It factors through A modulo one plus i.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.440
If that quotient were the zero ring,
it could not map one to a nonzero one.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.260
Therefore our quotient is nonzero
and has characteristic two.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.860
We do not need it to be a field.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.120
Finally, carry the inverse
identity through reduction.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:45.300
The images of x sub S and its
inverse still multiply to one.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:48.640
All our evaluated
denominators remain units.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:52.280
We can divide by c sub S in
this single quotient ring.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:56.300
Both two and one plus i become zero
there, so we cannot divide by them.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:00.720
The exact polynomial quotients involving
them were already formed before reduction.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.040
Keep this order of operations
throughout the next comparisons.
